{red|**Think about what might be missing in terms of shared purpose in your marriage.**

Do you know what you are building together?}{red| Do you have goals you are working toward as a couple?}{red| Do you talk about the future with excitement or just anxiety?}{red| Does daily life feel connected to something larger?}

Write about what feels missing or unclear regarding shared vision in your marriage:

{red|**Sometimes dreams need to be recovered.** They may have been buried under layers of practicality, disappointment, or neglect.

To recover your dreams, ask yourself: What did you dream about when you were younger before life taught you to be realistic?}{red| What activities make you lose track of time?}{red| What makes you feel most alive?}{red| What would you do if money and time were not obstacles?}{red| What breaks your heart about the world?}{red| What do you secretly wish you could try?}

**Your answers reveal desires that may have been dormant.** Not all of them will become part of your shared vision. But knowing them helps you understand what you bring to the conversation.

{red|**Take time to explore your own dreams across different areas.**

What do you want for your marriage and family?}{red| What do you want to accomplish vocationally?}{red| What experiences do you want to have?}{red| Who do you want to become?}{red| What impact do you want to make?}{red| What legacy do you want to leave?}

Write freely about your dreams without filtering for practicality:

{red|**Reflect on your own core values.**

What principles guide your decisions?}{red| What would you sacrifice for?}{red| What cannot be compromised?}{red| What do you want to be known for?}

List your top five to seven core values in order of importance:

{red|**It can help to think about marriages you admire.**

What couples do you look at and think: I want a marriage like that?}{red| What specifically do you admire about them?}{red| Is it how they communicate?}{red| Their obvious affection?}{red| Their teamwork?}{red| Their fun together?}

{red|**Michael and Janet identified three couples they admired.** One couple had been married fifty years and still held hands everywhere. Another couple ran a nonprofit together with obvious mutual respect. A third couple had navigated serious illness while their love only deepened. "We took notes," Michael said. "We asked ourselves what we could learn from each of them."

**Also consider anti-models.** What marriages have you observed that you definitely do not want to replicate?}{red| What specifically do you want to avoid?}

**These positive and negative examples help clarify your relationship vision.** You are not copying anyone else's marriage. But observing others helps you see what is possible and what to pursue or avoid.

{red|**Envision your ideal marriage.**

What do you want your marriage to feel like day to day?}{red| How do you want to treat each other?}{red| What do you want to be known for as a couple?}{red| What would make you look back in twenty years and say: We built something beautiful?}

Describe your vision for your marriage relationship itself:

{red|**Envision the life you want to build together across key areas.**

Home: Where and how do you want to live?}{red| Family: What do you want your family to look like?}{red| Work: What do you want to accomplish vocationally?}{red| Finances: What financial position do you want to achieve?}{red| Community: What relationships and connections do you want?}{red| Impact: How do you want to serve and contribute?}{red| Experiences: What do you want to do and enjoy?}

Write your vision for your shared life:

{red|**Based on your shared vision, brainstorm potential goals.**

What specific objectives would move you toward your vision in the next year?}{red| In the next five years?}

{red|What relationship goals would strengthen your marriage?}{red| What family goals would build the family you want?}{red| What financial goals would create security or generosity?}{red| What other goals matter to you?}

List potential goals without filtering yet:

{red|**Honestly assess how well your daily life aligns with your vision and goals.**

Where does your time actually go?}{red| Does it match what you say matters?}{red| Where does your money actually go?}{red| Does it reflect your values?}{red| What daily habits support your vision?}{red| Which ones undermine it?}{red| What is the biggest gap between your stated vision and your actual life?}

Write about your alignment assessment:

{red|**Reflect on obstacles you face or anticipate in pursuing your shared vision.**

What challenges currently stand in the way of your vision?}{red| What setbacks have you experienced that affected your direction?}{red| What obstacles do you anticipate in the future?}{red| How have you historically responded to obstacles in your marriage?}

Write about the obstacles you face:

{red|**Discuss with your spouse how you will handle obstacles to your vision.**

**Identify two or three likely obstacles you may face.**

For each, discuss: How will we recognize if this becomes a real challenge?}{red| How will we adapt our approach if needed?}{red| What will we not compromise even if this happens?}

{red|Also discuss: How do we want to process setbacks when they occur?}{red| How can we support each other during difficult times?}

**Write your obstacle response plan.**

{red|**Reflect on the legacy you want to leave.**

What do you want people to say about you and your marriage when you are gone?}{red| What impact do you want to have on your children or others you influence?}{red| What values do you want to pass on?}{red| What do you want to still exist because you lived?}

Describe the legacy you want to build together:

{red|What is the most important thing you have learned or realized through this journey about shared vision and purpose?}

{red|What do you want to remember and apply going forward?}

